WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.060
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book one.

2
00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:09.083
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.383 --> 00:00:17.318
Unit three: Philosophical Inquiry. Today you will explore abstract possibilities with inverted hypothetical conditionals.

4
00:00:17.618 --> 00:00:20.305
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.267 --> 00:00:24.767
Part one: words. You will learn ten new words.

6
00:00:25.617 --> 00:00:26.838
Epistemology.

7
00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:32.044
Epistemology means the branch of philosophy concerned with knowledge and belief.

8
00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:36.240
Epistemology asks how we can know anything with certainty.

9
00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:39.932
Your turn. Say: Epistemology.

10
00:00:42.767 --> 00:00:44.000
Epistemology.

11
00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:45.995
Metaphysics.

12
00:00:46.983 --> 00:00:50.655
Metaphysics means the study of the fundamental nature of reality.

13
00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:54.585
Metaphysics questions whether time truly exists.

14
00:00:55.183 --> 00:00:58.021
Your turn. Say: Metaphysics.

15
00:01:00.867 --> 00:01:02.216
Metaphysics.

16
00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:04.369
Existentialism.

17
00:01:05.383 --> 00:01:10.552
Existentialism means a philosophy stressing individual freedom and the creation of meaning.

18
00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:14.717
Existentialism holds that we define ourselves through our choices.

19
00:01:15.317 --> 00:01:17.934
Your turn. Say: Existentialism.

20
00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:22.161
Existentialism.

21
00:01:22.917 --> 00:01:24.127
Ontology.

22
00:01:25.117 --> 00:01:28.579
Ontology means the study of what exists and the nature of being.

23
00:01:29.183 --> 00:01:33.157
The ontology of numbers puzzles mathematicians and philosophers alike.

24
00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:36.402
Your turn. Say: Ontology.

25
00:01:39.267 --> 00:01:40.767
Ontology.

26
00:01:41.517 --> 00:01:42.587
Paradox.

27
00:01:43.583 --> 00:01:47.812
Paradox means a statement that seems self-contradictory yet may be true.

28
00:01:48.417 --> 00:01:52.053
The ship of Theseus is a famous paradox about identity.

29
00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:55.314
Your turn. Say: Paradox.

30
00:01:58.167 --> 00:01:59.272
Paradox.

31
00:02:00.017 --> 00:02:01.169
Skepticism.

32
00:02:02.183 --> 00:02:07.399
Skepticism means the philosophical position that certain knowledge is impossible or doubtful.

33
00:02:07.983 --> 00:02:11.724
Descartes used radical skepticism to find a certain foundation.

34
00:02:12.317 --> 00:02:15.422
Your turn. Say: Skepticism.

35
00:02:18.267 --> 00:02:19.558
Skepticism.

36
00:02:20.317 --> 00:02:21.562
Consciousness.

37
00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:26.279
Consciousness means the state of being aware of oneself and the world.

38
00:02:26.883 --> 00:02:30.706
Science still cannot explain how consciousness arises from matter.

39
00:02:31.317 --> 00:02:34.480
Your turn. Say: Consciousness.

40
00:02:37.333 --> 00:02:38.311
Consciousness.

41
00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:39.981
Inquiry.

42
00:02:40.983 --> 00:02:44.504
Inquiry means a systematic investigation into a question.

43
00:02:45.117 --> 00:02:48.834
Philosophical inquiry begins with a carefully framed question.

44
00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:52.102
Your turn. Say: Inquiry.

45
00:02:54.967 --> 00:02:55.898
Inquiry.

46
00:02:56.650 --> 00:02:57.930
Phenomenology.

47
00:02:58.917 --> 00:03:03.169
Phenomenology means the study of experience as it appears to consciousness.

48
00:03:03.783 --> 00:03:07.304
Phenomenology describes what it is like to perceive a sunrise.

49
00:03:07.917 --> 00:03:11.242
Your turn. Say: Phenomenology.

50
00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:15.368
Phenomenology.

51
00:03:16.117 --> 00:03:17.176
Dialectic.

52
00:03:18.183 --> 00:03:22.505
Dialectic means reasoning that progresses through the clash of opposing ideas.

53
00:03:23.117 --> 00:03:26.916
Through dialectic, thesis and antithesis produce a new synthesis.

54
00:03:27.517 --> 00:03:30.332
Your turn. Say: Dialectic.

55
00:03:33.167 --> 00:03:34.272
Dialectic.

56
00:03:35.067 --> 00:03:38.799
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

57
00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:45.191
Visal, if a robot could perfectly imitate human conversation, would it actually be conscious?

58
00:03:46.283 --> 00:03:57.423
That depends on what consciousness is. Were behaviour sufficient, the robot would qualify; but many philosophers insist there must also be inner experience.

59
00:03:58.517 --> 00:04:01.178
How could we ever test for inner experience?

60
00:04:02.283 --> 00:04:13.086
That's precisely the problem. Were we able to measure subjective experience directly, the debate would be settled, but we can only observe behaviour.

61
00:04:14.183 --> 00:04:16.380
So the question may be unanswerable?

62
00:04:17.483 --> 00:04:27.183
Perhaps for now. Yet were philosophers to stop asking it, science would lose the conceptual clarity it needs to make progress.

63
00:04:28.333 --> 00:04:30.077
Part three: grammar.

64
00:04:30.917 --> 00:04:34.100
Grammar focus: Hypothetical abstraction.

65
00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:35.564
Look at the pattern.

66
00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:40.107
Were consciousness fully understood, many paradoxes would dissolve.

67
00:04:41.017 --> 00:04:44.537
Were behaviour sufficient, the robot would count as conscious.

68
00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:48.761
Were philosophers to stop asking, science would lose clarity.

69
00:04:49.700 --> 00:05:01.527
Formal inversion replaces 'if': Were X understood = If X were understood; Were we to stop = If we stopped. Use 'were' for all persons. Negative: Were it not true… (never 'Weren't it').

70
00:05:02.283 --> 00:05:06.224
Be careful! Don't say: Was the theory true, we would know.

71
00:05:08.167 --> 00:05:10.631
Say: Were the theory true, we would know.

72
00:05:10.931 --> 00:05:13.302
Inversion uses 'were' for all persons.

73
00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:16.706
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

74
00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:18.733
The Hard Problem.

75
00:05:19.033 --> 00:05:25.584
Philosophical inquiry differs from empirical science in that it examines the concepts on which all investigation depends.

76
00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:33.394
Epistemology asks what justifies belief, while metaphysics and ontology ask what kinds of things genuinely exist.

77
00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:43.110
Consciousness poses perhaps the most stubborn puzzle: were every neuron mapped, we might still be unable to explain why experience feels like anything at all.

78
00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:50.616
David Chalmers called this the 'hard problem', distinguishing it from the comparatively 'easy' problems of explaining behaviour.

79
00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:58.499
Were it ever solved, the solution would transform not only philosophy but also medicine, ethics and artificial intelligence.

80
00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:03.462
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

81
00:06:04.317 --> 00:06:07.466
blank the theory correct, the paradox would disappear.

82
00:06:07.766 --> 00:06:10.804
A, Was. B, Were. C, If were.

83
00:06:14.367 --> 00:06:15.890
The answer is B: Were.

84
00:06:17.050 --> 00:06:20.350
The study of what exists and the nature of being is blank.

85
00:06:20.650 --> 00:06:25.290
A, ontology. B, skepticism. C, dialectic.

86
00:06:28.833 --> 00:06:31.077
The answer is A: ontology.

87
00:06:32.217 --> 00:06:35.946
A statement that seems self-contradictory yet may be true is a blank.

88
00:06:36.246 --> 00:06:40.689
A, phenomenology. B, consciousness. C, paradox.

89
00:06:44.233 --> 00:06:46.558
The answer is C: paradox.

90
00:06:47.717 --> 00:06:49.507
Choose the correct sentence.

91
00:06:49.807 --> 00:06:57.106
A, Were we to agree, the debate would end.. B, Were we agree, the debate would end.. C, If were we to agree, the debate would end..

92
00:07:00.667 --> 00:07:03.688
The answer is A: Were we to agree, the debate would end..

93
00:07:04.850 --> 00:07:08.057
According to the reading, the 'hard problem' concerns…

94
00:07:08.357 --> 00:07:14.205
A, explaining behaviour. B, why experience feels like anything. C, mapping neurons.

95
00:07:17.767 --> 00:07:21.264
The answer is B: why experience feels like anything.

96
00:07:22.467 --> 00:07:25.897
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

97
00:07:26.750 --> 00:07:30.120
What philosophical question do you find most compelling?

98
00:07:46.917 --> 00:07:48.324
Now listen to Visal.

99
00:07:48.624 --> 00:08:22.782
The question I find most compelling is whether free will genuinely exists. Were all our choices determined by genes, upbringing and brain chemistry, moral responsibility would seem difficult to justify. Yet we experience ourselves as deciding, and legal systems assume that people could have acted otherwise. This tension fascinates me because it links metaphysics to justice. Were it resolved, our understanding of praise, blame and even education would change profoundly.

100
00:08:23.082 --> 00:08:24.745
Excellent answer, Visal!

101
00:08:25.650 --> 00:08:30.032
Great work! Now you can use ten new words and hypothetical abstraction.

102
00:08:30.332 --> 00:08:35.365
Today's value is wisdom. Think carefully and listen to good advice before you decide.

103
00:08:35.665 --> 00:08:38.143
See you in Unit four. Goodbye!

104
00:08:38.443 --> 00:08:41.687
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

